 for bestpractices and # Agent Trace Viewer

## Overview
Develop the Agent Trace Viewer as part of LifeOps’ Modular Multi-Agent AI Operating System. This page provides a visualizeable, interactive timeline of agent-to-agent communications within a single run, capturing handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus points. It deeply integrates with the Conflict Resolution & Policy Engine, Scoped Shared Memory & Vector DB, and the Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer. The UI enables filtering, drill-downs, export, and navigation to related artifacts and run details. The implementation must be robust to null/undefined values, guard all array operations, and initialize state with proper defaults to ensure runtime safety.

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- The Agent Trace Viewer is a specialized dashboard that renders a visual timeline of all agent interactions for a given run. It highlights agent-to-agent messages, handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus points, with expandable nodes for each event that show human-readable justifications, involved agents, action types, timing, and result.

Goals:
- Provide an at-a-glance timeline with interactive zooming/panning, grouping by agent or topic, and quick access to event details.
- Correlate events with outcomes, policies applied, and any conflicts resolved by the Policy Engine.
- Surface human-readable justifications for decisions, with links to relevant policy rules and run artifacts.
- Enable exporting of the trace as JSON and a human-readable report, and link to associated run artifacts and audit entries.

Connected features & components:
- Conflict Resolution & Policy Engine: Display policy decisions, precedence, and justifications per conflict event; allow toggling between raw rule evaluation and human-readable explanations.
- Scoped Shared Memory & Vector DB: Show memory reads/writes that influenced events; provide scoped memory details and TTL status; allow quick navigation to memory entries and their read/write contexts.
- Multi-Agent Orchestration Layer: Show scheduling events, handoffs, and negotiations; reflect states like pending, approved, or aborted.
- Agent Console (linked): Quick navigation to the originating agent’s config, permissions, and memory snapshot.
- Run Detail (linked): Provide a gateway to full run inputs/outputs, inter-agent trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, and revert actions.

UI elements & visual guidance:
- Interactive timeline canvas or list with expandable event nodes.
- Filters: agent, topic, rule violation type, time range, severity of alerts, and consensus vs. non-consensus events.
- Export: JSON export of the trace; human-readable PDF/HTML report export.
- Artifact links: quick access to run artifacts, diffs, logs, and audit entries.
- Visual cues: color-coded event types, status icons, and tooltips with concise summaries and deeper details.
- Keyboard accessibility: focus management, arrow navigation, and screen reader-friendly ARIA labels.

Visual guidance:
- Prefer flat, dark-mode-first UI with high-contrast accents; maintain a 12-column grid with generous spacing and consistent card-based sections.
- Use neon/bright accents for timeline markers to ensure readability on dark backgrounds.
- Use collapsible panels for event detail sections; provide a “collapse all/expand all” control for the event tree.

## Components to Build
- AgentTraceViewerPage
  - Orchestrates data fetch, state, and child components.
  - Handles run selection, date/time range, and global filters.
- TimelineCanvas or TimelineViewport
  - Renders events as interconnected nodes along a horizontal time axis.
  - Supports panning/zooming, tooltips, and click-to-expand.
  - Guard all array operations with Array.isArray checks and defaults.
- EventNode
  - Represents a single event (message, handoff, negotiation, alert, consensus).
  - Expandable content with details: involved agents, action type, timestamp, policy rule(s) applied, justification, memory effects, and artifacts.
- FilterPanel
  - Multi-selects and sliders for agent, topic, event type, severity, time range.
- DetailPanel
  - Side panel or bottom sheet showing event-specific details, including:
    - Rule engine justification text
    - Memory read/write context
    - Handoff/Negotiation transcripts
    - Linked run artifacts and audit entries
- ExportPanel
  - Buttons and dialogs for JSON export and human-readable report generation.
- LinkedNavigationWidgets
  - Quick links to Agent Console, Run Detail, and related artifacts.
- MemorySliceInspector
  - Inlined view of scoped memory entries read/written by the event, with TTL status and access controls.
- PolicyEngineInsights
  - Summarizes conflicts, precedence resolution, and human-readable justifications per event.
- ActivityLegend
  - Color legend for event types, status, and priority.
- API Integration Layer
  - Data adapters that fetch run trace data, policy decisions, memory entries, and orchestration events.

Technical details:
- Data presentation must rely on data shapes provided by the backend (see Data Models). Do not assume non-null arrays; guard all accesses.

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Frameworks: React + TypeScript (recommended), leveraging a modern state management approach (e.g., React Context, Zustand, or Redux Toolkit).
- State initialization:
  - useState<T[]>([]) for arrays (agents, events, artifacts, etc.).
  - useState<RunTrace | null>(null) for the current trace, with safe conditional rendering.
- Data fetching:
  - Use fetch/axios or your preferred client with proper error handling.
  - Ensure all API results use nullish coalescing: const items = data ?? [].
  - Validate API shapes: const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [].
- Safety: Guard array methods:
  - (events ?? []).map(...), (events ?? []).filter(...), etc.
  - If computing on nested structures, use Array.isArray checks before mapping.
- UI interactions:
  - Timeline supports keyboard navigation and accessible tooltips.
  - Expandable EventDetail sections with no more than 1 level of nesting by default, with option to expand deeper.
- Accessibility:
  - ARIA roles for timeline, events, and panels.
  - High-contrast focus outlines in the accent color.
- API contracts:
  - API endpoints and data contracts must be TypeScript-typed, with runtime validation.

### Backend
- Not required to build APIs from scratch in this prompt, but define and describe endpoints that would be used by frontend:
  - GET /runs/{runId}/trace
  - GET /runs/{runId}/trace/{eventId}
  - GET /runs/{runId}/artifacts
  - GET /agents/{agentId}/config
  - GET /policies/{policyId}/justification
  - GET /memory/{memoryId}
  - GET /memory?runId=&scope=&agentId=
- Database considerations:
  - Data models must reflect:
    - Event types: message, handoff, negotiation, alert, consensus
    - Policy engine results: rules, precedence, justification
    - Memory reads/writes with TTLs and access controls
    - Agent orchestration events: schedule, route, handoff, etc.
  - Defensive measures: allow null values in API responses; backend should coerce to safe defaults or provide empty arrays.
- Security: JWT-based authentication and authorization per user role; ensure data returned is scoped to authorized runs/agents.

### Integration
- Data flow:
  - Agent Trace Viewer fetches run trace data from /runs/{runId}/trace.
  - For each event, fetch related policy justification and memory context as needed (with caching).
  - Provide links to Agent Console and Run Detail via IDs present in events.
- State synchronization:
  - Centralized runId parameter across components to fetch/refresh trace data.
  - Debounce filters to avoid excessive API calls; apply pagination if results are large.
- Error handling:
  - Centralized error boundary for the page with user-friendly messages.
  - Graceful fallback: if certain related data is missing, show placeholders and explain the missing links.

## User Experience Flow
1. User opens the Agent Trace Viewer for a specific run.
2. Timeline loads with events: messages, handoffs, negotiations, alerts, and consensus points.
3. User uses filters to isolate events by agent, topic, or rule violation type.
4. User clicks an event node to expand details; the panel shows:
   - Event type, timestamp, agents involved
   - Policy rules applied and justification
   - Memory reads/writes implicated, with TTL status
   - Handoff/negotiation transcripts
   - Links to artifacts and audit entries
5. User toggles to view policy engine insights for conflicts and resolutions.
6. User exports trace as JSON or a human-readable report.
7. User navigates to related pages (Agent Console, Run Detail) for deeper inspection.
8. Optional: User can apply quick filters to see only consensus points or only high-severity alerts.

## Technical Specifications

Data Models (Schema details)
- RunTrace
  - runId: string
  - events: Event[]
  - artifacts: Artifact[]
  - summary: string
- Event
  - eventId: string
  - type: 'message' | 'handoff' | 'negotiation' | 'alert' | 'consensus'
  - timestamp: string (ISO 8601)
  - fromAgentId: string | null
  - toAgentId: string | null
  - topic?: string
  - details?: any (object with event-specific payload)
  - policyResultId?: string
  - memoryAccessIds?: string[]
  - linkedRunArtifactIds?: string[]
- PolicyResult
  - policyResultId: string
  - appliedRules: RuleOutcome[]
- RuleOutcome
  - ruleId: string
  - result: 'allow' | 'deny' | 'modify'
  - precedenceRank: number
  - justification: string
- MemoryAccess
  - memoryId: string
  - accessType: 'read' | 'write'
  - agentId: string
  - ttlSeconds?: number
  - scope: string
  - valueSnapshot?: any
- MemoryEntry
  - memoryId: string
  - scope: string
  - ownerAgentId: string
  - value: any
  - ttl: string | null
  - accessControls: string[]
- Agent
  - agentId: string
  - name: string
  - capabilities: string[]
  - config: any
- Artifact
  - artifactId: string
  - type: string
  - name: string
  - url: string
  - metadata?: any

API Endpoints (Routes and methods)
- GET /runs/{runId}/trace
  - Query: filters, timeRange, limit, offset
  - Response: { events: Event[], summary: string, runId: string, ... }
- GET /runs/{runId}/trace/{eventId}
  - Response: Event detail with policyResult and related memoryAccess
- GET /runs/{runId}/artifacts
  - Response: Artifact[]
- GET /agents/{agentId}/config
  - Response: Agent config object
- GET /policies/{policyResultId}
  - Response: PolicyResult
- GET /memory/{memoryId}
  - Response: MemoryEntry
- GET /memory?runId=&scope=&agentId=
  - Response: MemoryEntry[]

Security
- Authentication: JWT tokens; role-based access; scopes per run/artifact/agent.
- Authorization: Ensure user can access the runId; validate tokens on every request.
- Input Validation: Validate all query parameters and path vars; sanitize outputs.

Validation
- Always validate API responses shapes:
  - const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [];
  - const runTrace = response?.data?.events ?? [];
- Guard against null: (events ?? []).map(...)
- Destructure with defaults:
  - const { events = [], summary = "" } = response ?? {}

Validation Rules
- The UI must not crash if any sub-collection is missing or empty.
- All event arrays must be rendered safely with checks for Array.isArray before mapping.
- All memory/artifact links must be enabled only if URLs are present; otherwise, show disabled state with tooltip.

Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] The Agent Trace Viewer renders a timeline with at least five event types (message, handoff, negotiation, alert, consensus) for a given run.
- [ ] Filtering by agent, topic, and event type updates the timeline in real-time without runtime errors, guarding all array operations.
- [ ] Expanding an event shows a detail panel with policy justification, memory context, and related artifacts; all data is guarded for null/undefined values.
- [ ] Export functionality works for both JSON and human-readable report; exports contain complete event data with safe defaults where fields are missing.
- [ ] Navigation from an event to the related Agent Console and Run Detail pages works and passes proper IDs via routes.
- [ ] All API responses are validated client-side; no crashes if some fields are missing or null.
- [ ] UI adheres to the design system: dark mode, 12-column grid, card-based sections, accessible controls, and color usage per the specified palette.

UI/UX Guidelines
Apply the project's design system:
- Maintain dark mode-first aesthetic with flat color blocks and depth via shadows.
- Use 12-column grid with 24–32px gutters and padding.
- Cards with rounded corners, subtle borders, hover states, bold titles, and bottom-anchored actions.
- Sidebar navigation with active state highlighting; top bar with search and quick actions.
- Data visualizations: neon-accent timelines and charts on dark backgrounds.
- Interactive elements: rounded buttons, large input fields, accessible focus states, smooth micro-interactions.

Visual Style

Color Palette:
- Primary background: #18191C
- Card/background containers: #232429, #1F2124
- Accent: #4F8CFF
- Status: #5ED36D (green), #FFD66C (yellow); Errors: #EF6464 (red)
- Text: White #FFFFFF; Secondary #AEB2B8; Placeholder #74777D
- Borders/dividers: #26282C and #232429 with 30–60% opacity

Typography & Layout:
- Font: Inter or similar
- Weights: 700 headings, 600 section titles, 400 body
- Layout: 12-column grid, generous spacing, left-aligned content
- Navigation: Pill-shaped active states, acrylic feel with color accents

Key Design Elements
- Card: 12–16px radius, inner shadows, hover glow
- Timeline: Neon accents, dots at data points, light gridlines
- Interactive elements: Primary blue buttons (#4F8CFF); form fields with soft borders
- Accessibility: Ensure contrast and keyboard navigability

Design Philosophy
- Modern, minimalist SaaS aesthetic; dark-first; modular, accessible
- Clear traceability and actionable overviews
- Reversible actions with audit trail

Mandatory Coding Standards — Runtime Safety

CRITICAL: Follow these rules in ALL generated code to prevent runtime crashes.

1. Supabase query results: Always use nullish coalescing — data ?? [].
2. Array methods: Never call on possibly null/undefined values. Use (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : [].
3. React useState for arrays/objects: Initialize with correct type, e.g., useState<Type[]>([]).
4. API response shapes: Validate — const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : [].
5. Optional chaining: Use obj?.property?.nested when accessing nested API data.
6. Destructuring with defaults: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}.

Connected Pages and Features
- Agent Console: Access agent config, permissions, memory snapshots, and run simulations.
- Run Detail: Full run details: inputs, outputs, inter-agent trace, logs, artifacts, diffs, errors, and revert actions.

Notes for AI Development Tool Prompting
- Provide explicit type definitions for all data models (TypeScript interfaces) and runtime validators.
- Emit code with defensive programming patterns that guard against undefined/null values before array operations.
- Include unit tests or test scaffolds that validate behavior with missing fields and empty arrays.
- Document API contracts and expected payload shapes within code comments for maintainability.
- Ensure that all components gracefully degrade when data is partial or missing; UI should clearly indicate missing data without breaking.

This prompt is designed to guide an AI development tool to generate a robust, safe, and design-compliant Agent Trace Viewer with integrated conflict resolution, memory store, and multi-agent orchestration insights. It emphasizes runtime safety, clear data contracts, and a cohesive UI aligned with LifeOps’ design system. for style.

CRITICAL DESIGN REQUIREMENTS:
- You MUST follow ALL design principles and patterns specified in the Design Reference above
- Do NOT deviate from the design specifications without explicit user permission
- Implement ALL visual design elements (colors, typography, spacing, shadows, animations) as specified
- Follow the layout patterns and component designs exactly as documented
- Ensure all interactions and micro-animations match the reference specifications

CRITICAL TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS:
- React 18.3.1, React Router 6.30.1
- Tailwind CSS v3 with tailwind.config.js and custom keyframes
- @tailwindcss/typography plugin for rich text content
- Tailwind CSS animations (NOT Motion library or framer-motion)
- Sonner for toasts
- Recharts for data visualization
- Native fetch() with API utilities in src/lib/api.ts
- Design system with CSS custom properties
- Use RGB color values for theming
- When Supabase is configured: use Edge Functions for LLM and server-only logic; never expose API keys in the client

